<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Arjuna</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Arjuna is a hero of the Indian epic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahabharata">Mahabharata</a>, the third of the five Pandava brothers and a peerless archer. His dialogue with his charioteer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krishna">Krishna</a> before the battle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kurukshetra">Kurukshetra</a> forms the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bhagavad-gita">Bhagavad Gita</a>. Peoples and kings claimed descent from him, among them the Arjunayanas of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajasthan">Rajasthan</a>, and poets such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adikavi-pampa">Pampa</a> cast their royal patrons in his role.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/arjuna.jpg" alt="Arjuna" width="320" height="479" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ilussion · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Arjuna_statue.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Within it stands the Bhagavad Gita, a dialogue on the battlefield between the warrior <b>Arjuna</b> and his charioteer Krishna, revealed as God, on duty, devotion and the nature of the self; it has become the best known of all Hindu texts.</blockquote><a href="/religion/hinduism">Hinduism · Texts &amp; Teachers</a></li><li><blockquote>…longest poems in the world, tells of the war between two branches of a royal family and contains the Bhagavad Gita, the dialogue between the warrior <b>Arjuna</b> and the god Krishna; the Ramayana, attributed to the sage Valmiki, tells the story of Prince Rama and his wife Sita.</blockquote><a href="/language/sanskrit">Sanskrit · Literature &amp; Learning</a></li><li><blockquote>…the throne of Hastinapura, ruled by the Kuru clan, between two branches of the family: the Kauravas, the hundred sons of the blind king Dhritarashtra led by Duryodhana, and the Pandavas, the five sons of Pandu, Yudhishthira, Bhima, <b>Arjuna</b>, Nakula and Sahadeva, who all marry the princess Draupadi.</blockquote><a href="/work/mahabharata">Mahabharata · What It Is</a></li><li><blockquote>They took their name from <b>Arjuna</b>, the Pandava hero of the Mahabharata, and appear to have emerged as an independent gana in the Bharatpur and Alwar region after the fall of the Maurya Empire.</blockquote><a href="/state/arjunayanas">Arjunayanas</a></li><li><blockquote>Arikesari II was the patron of the poet Pampa, who wrote the Kannada epics Vikramarjuna Vijaya and Adipurana in 941 and cast Arikesari as the hero <b>Arjuna</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/chalukyas-of-vemulavada">Chalukyas of Vemulavada</a></li><li><blockquote>Kings such as Yolamira and <b>Arjuna</b> are known only by name.</blockquote><a href="/state/paratarajas">Paratarajas</a></li></ul></section>
